A||||17||DJRice||60* V-6s and oil delivery||||4||
Z||000000||DJRice||06-06-2001||08:22 PM||||After recently dealing with a spun bearing problem on my brothers car, I am wondering is there a model year and/or engine size (i.e. 1990 3.1L)where GM finally improved the oil flow thru the block/crank/etc.?  I figure this information could be useful in the future to anyone who is trying to make the decision to rebuild a 2.8 vs. swap to the 3.1L or 3.4L.    <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/djr_signature.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000001||fiero56||06-06-2001||08:49 PM||||'88 and later 60 degree blocks have better oiling system.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||06-06-2001||08:52 PM||||Before you rebuild one, especially punching it out to a 3.1/3.4... check into GM's crates. Cost of rebuilding and debugging might not be much less than buying a crate with a warantee. Then if you fry a rod bearing it's covered.... Plus the crate motor comes ready to drop in and go. Depends just how you order it.<P>There are probably aftermarket crates of this motor as well. After market gives you even more options and still gives a warantee...<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Dave Gunsul||06-06-2001||09:02 PM||||You might also want to include a windage tray<BR>(Fiero store sells one), and a high vol. oil<BR>pump. these items will help your new engine last much longer.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||06-06-2001||09:17 PM||||Fiero56, what is the oiling improvement in the '88s?  I've never heard of this before.  The '85 and later engines have the 67 mm mains (vs the 63 mm mains) and in '87 the internally balanced crankshafts were used in production.  Also aside from those changes, the lead in oil groove was introduced in '87 but I'm not aware of actual changes in the block to improve oiling.<P>TIA||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black88GT||06-06-2001||09:19 PM||||I don't know how true this is, but I heard that if you drill the starter holes on the opposite side of the block, requirement when you order the 3.4 crate motor, that you void the warranty.  This sounds funny to me, might just be a rumor, but I would definitely check into it if you are considering the 3.4 crate motor.||||reg||1
Z||000006||PJ||06-06-2001||09:41 PM||||Dillon,<BR>As far as I know they all oil the same. First lifters then cam then mains then rods. The rod journals are supplied oil through the mains. There is only one hole in the main journal that leads to the rod. Only the top main bearing half is grooved so over half the time no oil is flowing to the rod journal. Currently I'm cross drilling a crank to try to alieviate the problem. The stock 2.8L oil pan is well baffled so its unlikely your pump would swallow air unless cornering was really extreme or your pickup is too high. GM fitted mid 90 3.1L's with a nice windage tray. Just a little beating, banging and grinding and it will work fine in a Fiero pan.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR>  <p>[This message has been edited by PJ (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||06-06-2001||11:05 PM||||87:<P>The lead in grooves are what I was referring to. In RWD applications, the internally balanced crank was introduced in '88, and I am not positive, but I the the grooves also first appeard in RWD in '88 too.||||reg||1
Z||000008||87GTZ34||06-06-2001||11:49 PM||||Thanks 56.  BTW - You can get more oil to flow by cross drilling the two center main bearings.  The engine only needs it during high revs.  If building up an engine with a goal of sustained high rpm range operation, plan on this step to avert dumping rod bearings.<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 06-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||hyhy4u||06-07-2001||12:39 AM||||Kind of on the subject....if your going to replace the engine, dont go with a early 90's GM 3.4, one of the worst engines GM ever made.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||06-07-2001||08:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by PJ:<BR><B>Dillon,<BR>As far as I know they all oil the same. First lifters then cam then mains then rods. The rod journals are supplied oil through the mains. There is only one hole in the main journal that leads to the rod. Only the top main bearing half is grooved so over half the time no oil is flowing to the rod journal. Currently I'm cross drilling a crank to try to alieviate the problem. The stock 2.8L oil pan is well baffled so its unlikely your pump would swallow air unless cornering was really extreme or your pickup is too high. GM fitted mid 90 3.1L's with a nice windage tray. Just a little beating, banging and grinding and it will work fine in a Fiero pan.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR>  <P>[This message has been edited by PJ (edited 06-06-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>My car has never to my knowledge had the pan removed, so everything shoudl be the way the factory assembled it.<BR>I loose oil pressure when drifting 270 degree on ramps if my oil is even a little bit low.<P>I have crappy tires right now, but I imagine oil starvation might be a problem with serious rubber.<P>They had that problem early in the development of the C5 and LS1. Test cars were blowing engines on the skidpad from oil starvation.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||loafer87gt||06-07-2001||10:54 AM||||Jeff: Thats right, I went to a couple of GM dealerships and was told the same thing, warranty is void if the starter holes are tapped. I even asked one of their service guys if they would honour the warranty if I gave them the jig so they could tap it themselves but nope. It really sucks because a 3.4 performance crate is only $2300 canadian here, less than the cost of a rebuild. ||||reg||1
